"""Cyber Explorers incident command manuscript; independent editorial review pending."""
COURSE = {
  "key": "explorers-command-v1",
  "title": "Digital Safety Response & Helping Others",
  "academy_id": "secondary",
  "pathway": "secondary",
  "stage": "command",
  "revision": "v1",
  "description": "Eight reading lessons covering incident triage, escalation, safe containment, communications, support, recovery and classroom tabletop response.",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "position": 1,
      "title": "Recognising an incident that needs help",
      "content": "Some events can be handled through ordinary verification; others require a coordinated response. A suspicious message without interaction is different from confirmed unauthorised account use or a payment that has already been sent. Classification should consider evidence, urgency, possible harm and the need for a responsible helper.\n\nWorked example: A classmate receives a fake school login message but does not open it. Another has entered a password on the unfamiliar page. The first reports the message; the second uses the legitimate service to recover access and checks other affected accounts.\n\n**Triage by consequence.** A message that was ignored does not justify the same response as one followed by a password submission. Classify the event by what actually happened and which account or person might be affected. If a learner merely saw a suspicious request, preserving a factual report and verifying the official source may be enough. If credentials were submitted, secure the account through the real provider and ask an authorised adult or support team for help. Record the evidence that supports each classification and avoid declaring that data was stolen without proof.\n\n**When the evidence changes.** Suppose the first learner later remembers approving an unfamiliar sign-in request. Their classification changes even if no visible harm has occurred. The second learner may see no strange devices in their session list, but that does not prove the entered credential was never captured. Explain the difference between an observed session, a submitted password and verified misuse. After official account-recovery steps, use provider-supported controls to review sessions and recovery methods. Record which response has been completed, which uncertainty remains and which trusted contact can confirm the next safe action. Do not attempt to investigate the fake page yourself.\n\n**A report that does not collect secrets.** Write what happened in ordinary language: 'I typed my school password into a page reached from an unexpected message at about four o'clock.' That is useful even without a screenshot. Do not write the password itself or ask a friend to test it. If the learner cannot remember whether a form was submitted, say so. A trusted helper can work with uncertainty; a confident but invented detail may send them down the wrong route. Having clicked something is not a reason to hide the event.\n\n**Three cases to sort.** Use these fictional facts on paper. Case A: a student reads the message preview and deletes it without opening anything. Case B: a student opens the disputed page, types a password and presses submit. Case C: a student reports an unfamiliar approval prompt, rejects it and later sees a provider notice saying a session was accepted. For each, write the observed action, one concern and the first trusted help route. Then decide which case needs the most prompt account-security attention and explain what is still unknown.\n\n**Explained answers.** A supports a message report, not a takeover claim. B establishes disclosure to the disputed form, making official account-security steps appropriate even if no misuse is visible. C contains evidence different from a rejected prompt alone: the accepted-session notice warrants prompt review through the actual provider. Confirm what the notice refers to rather than using its embedded contact details. None identifies the person behind the message. Your classification should guide practical assistance rather than award the most frightening label.\n\n**Check the handoff.** A report is not finished merely because it was sent. Note whether the appropriate helper received it, what the learner should do while waiting and when to follow up. A student should not take responsibility for inspecting every school account. If the account belongs to the school, the school's authorised support team decides wider changes. Reflection: Which new fact would move A into a different response category? A later admission of entering a secret changes the exposure; another copy of the same message does not. The takeaway is to sort the action that actually occurred, ask for suitable help and revise the response when the facts change."
    },
    {
      "position": 2,
      "title": "Choosing the right escalation route",
      "content": "A cyber concern should reach someone who can act. School IT may manage student accounts, a payment provider can investigate a transfer, a social platform can review an abusive account and a trusted adult can help a younger learner seek support. The contact supplied inside an unexpected suspicious message is not necessarily trustworthy.\n\nWorked example: A student receives a demand for an account recovery fee. They independently access the school support page and ask whether a fee exists. Another student who transferred money contacts the payment provider promptly through its established channel.\n\n**Choosing a responsible contact.** A fake timetable notification needs verification through the school's established academic channel. An unexpected account lockout belongs with the authorised portal-support staff; harassment and immediate threats may require a safeguarding adult. A classmate can help the learner find public contact information but must not ask to receive passwords or login codes. For each case, explain who has the relevant authority and what the learner can safely do while waiting. A useful escalation route includes a backup contact if the first person is unavailable rather than assuming every problem belongs with the nearest teacher.\n\n**When the first route fails.** Imagine a teacher confirms that a timetable notice is not genuine but cannot inspect student portal logs. The next response is a documented referral to the portal administrator, not repeated forwarding of student credentials. Record who owns the next step and how the learner will learn whether access is restored. If the situation includes a credible threat to the student's immediate safety, use the school's safeguarding procedure rather than treating it as an ordinary technical support ticket. Explain how the escalation plan changes without turning every unusual email into an emergency.\n\n**Say what you need from the recipient.** 'Something strange happened' gives a helper little to work with. A useful request names the affected service, approximate time, action taken and help needed. For an account problem, ask how to secure or regain access. For a payment, ask the payment provider about the transaction and its available dispute process. Reporting quickly may help, but neither a friend nor a teacher can guarantee a refund. A threatening post may require platform reporting and personal support at the same time.\n\n**Independent practice.** Match three invented reports to their next recipients. One student cannot sign in to a school-managed mailbox after sharing a password. Another sent a transfer requested by an impersonated seller. A third is frightened by repeated threatening messages. For each, choose a primary contact, a supportive adult if appropriate and a backup route if the first contact is unavailable. Describe how you would find the route without using the suspicious sender's number. This is a paper directory; do not enter real account details or contact people merely to test the exercise.\n\n**Reasoned review.** The mailbox belongs with school account support; the transfer belongs with the institution or provider handling that payment. The threatening messages justify help from a trusted adult and the appropriate platform safety process. Immediate danger needs urgent local help rather than an unanswered email queue. Some cases need more than one recipient because technical access and personal safety are different needs. Explain the purpose of each handoff so the learner is not sent endlessly from one person to another.\n\n**Follow-up without public pressure.** Keep a private note of the attempted contact, any non-secret case reference and the agreed next update. Use another route independently listed by the same organisation when necessary. A social profile that approaches after seeing a complaint is not automatically official support. Do not post transaction details or identity documents to attract a faster answer. Reflection: If a helpful adult lacks authority to reset the account, what can they still do? They can help find and reach the correct service, explain instructions and stay with the learner during the handoff. Escalation means getting the right help, not surrendering control to whoever answers first."
    },
    {
      "position": 3,
      "title": "Containing harm without making it worse",
      "content": "Containment means reducing an ongoing problem while preserving legitimate activity as much as practical. A user can stop engaging with a suspicious sender, revoke an unfamiliar account session or request help with an affected school device. The response should not automatically involve deleting evidence, sharing a suspicious link or switching off every protective control.\n\nWorked example: A learner notices an unfamiliar device on the account's session list. They revoke it through genuine settings and review recovery methods. Another learner receives an alert but has no evidence of a successful unknown session; they avoid making unsupported claims while checking.\n\n**Containment versus deletion.** The purpose of containment is to limit possible ongoing harm while protecting the information needed to understand the event. If an unfamiliar session is confirmed in official account settings, revoking that session may reduce exposure. Deleting the whole account or resetting a school-owned device without permission may destroy important work and remove evidence. Describe what can be done safely by the account holder and which wider intervention belongs to the responsible service owner.\n\n**A disrupted class calendar.** Suppose an administrator removes too many permissions while investigating an unfamiliar session. Students can no longer see a shared deadline. Record the new availability problem separately from the account concern, and ask the authorised owner to restore only the legitimate access needed. The response should retain a record of what was changed and why. Compare the result with the original threat: an effective containment action reduces exposure while preserving a sensible route back to ordinary work. The class should not try to bypass the permission change with shared administrator credentials.\n\n**Name the harm you are trying to limit.** If somebody may be using your account, the goal is to stop their access while keeping a safe way for you to return. If a suspicious sender keeps contacting you, the goal may be to stop interaction and report the approach. If a school-owned computer behaves strangely, the owner needs to decide the next technical step. These goals suggest different actions. A factory reset is not a universal answer, and an account password change does not repair a damaged file.\n\n**A small decision table.** On paper, compare three proposals for a fictional account incident: use the provider's sign-out control for unfamiliar sessions, delete every shared class document, or ask classmates to use one shared replacement password. Record the exposure each proposal addresses and any new harm it creates. No real settings need changing. Add a check that the rightful owner can still reach coursework through their own account after the protective step. If a setting is unclear, use the provider's guidance or authorised school support rather than guessing.\n\n**Explained choice.** Provider-supported session controls can address ongoing account access where that feature is available. Indiscriminate document deletion does not remove another person's session and can destroy classmates' work. A shared replacement password creates another account-control problem and obscures who is using the service. Following a password change, confirm how the provider handles existing sessions instead of assuming they all ended. A sensible containment note says which action occurred and which account check remains, without claiming every possible risk has disappeared.\n\n**Choose an end condition.** A temporary restriction should have a reason to remain and an appropriate person to review it. For the class calendar, staff might restore ordinary read access after checking the legitimate group permissions while keeping administrative changes restricted. Students should not bypass that restriction to hurry the process. If classmates need a deadline meanwhile, ask the responsible teacher for an approved notice. That keeps the real schoolwork moving without inventing another unofficial calendar.\n\nReflection: Suppose the first explanation turns out to be mistaken. Which action would leave the class best able to recover? Compare the reversible session review with deleting the only copy of a group assignment. The lesson's takeaway is proportionate protection: stop the relevant exposure, retain useful information and preserve an authorised route back to ordinary activity."
    },
    {
      "position": 4,
      "title": "Communicating clearly during a problem",
      "content": "During an incident people want certainty, but an accurate update may need to explain what is still unknown. A useful message states the observed event, affected service, action taken, next verification step and trusted contact. It should not identify an attacker without evidence or publish private information about classmates.\n\nWorked example: A study group incorrectly announces that all school accounts have been hacked after a few people see password-reset messages. A representative checks with school IT and shares a corrected advisory that describes the actual known issue and appropriate response.\n\n**An update must enable action.** Suppose the school portal is temporarily unavailable for file uploads. A useful notice explains the confirmed affected function, a permitted alternative submission route and when learners should check the official page again. It avoids blaming a person before an investigation establishes facts. Compare that message with 'the system has been hacked, do not use anything,' which could mislead students about unrelated services. Decide what the audience needs now and which questions are better handled privately by authorised support staff. Where facts remain uncertain, say so clearly rather than inventing a recovery time.\n\n**Correct the record promptly.** Imagine a first report says the whole portal is unavailable, but testing later confirms that lessons remain readable and only uploads are failing. Publish the correction in the same school channel, explain what changed and retain the safe workaround. Do not conceal the earlier error or claim that the service is fully recovered until the affected operation has been retested. Keep private incident details out of the public explanation. Students should learn that accurate updates can evolve as evidence improves and that uncertainty is better than unsupported certainty.\n\n**Facts for your own writing task.** At 15:00, two fictional learners cannot upload an assignment. Reading lessons still works. At 15:10, the teacher confirms that work may temporarily be handed in through the school's usual homework desk; nobody should invent a new email address or public file-sharing folder. A further update is due on the established class noticeboard at 16:00. There is no confirmed explanation for the upload problem. These facts are enough to practise the task independently; do not add an attacker or an estimated repair time.\n\n**Draft for two purposes.** Write a short group notice with the affected function, approved temporary route and next update. Then write a private support note stating the approximate start time, what the learners tried and what error they saw. Use fictional names or roles, and do not include passwords or screenshots of private accounts. The private note can be more specific without becoming a copy of every student's messages. The public notice needs a safe next action, not all the supporting material.\n\n**An explained example.** A suitable notice is: 'Assignment uploads are currently failing; lessons remain available. The teacher has approved the usual homework desk for today's submissions. Check the class noticeboard for the next update at 16:00.' This tells people what to do and limits the claim to the observed function. 'All school systems have been hacked' is unsupported by the facts. 'Everything is fixed' would also be unsupported merely because reading still works.\n\n**Read it as a worried classmate.** Could somebody mistake the workaround for a permanent policy? Could the notice send them to an unverified contact? Would they know when and where to look for a correction? Add the teacher's approved time limit if one is supplied; otherwise ask rather than inventing it. You may read the notice aloud to yourself if no partner is available. Reflection: If uploads work again for one learner, can you announce complete recovery immediately? Report that observation to the responsible staff and wait for the agreed confirmation. Clear updates connect each claim to its evidence and make changes visible in the same channel where people saw the earlier advice."
    },
    {
      "position": 5,
      "title": "Supporting a friend without taking over",
      "content": "A helpful peer respects the affected person's agency. They can explain official recovery instructions, find the right support contact and help keep factual notes, but should not demand passwords, authentication codes or unfettered account access. Threats, embarrassment and online pressure require compassion and possibly safeguarding assistance.\n\nWorked example: A friend is frightened by a message claiming private photographs will be published. A classmate avoids spreading the message, helps identify a trusted adult and approved platform reporting route, and does not pressure the friend to reveal more personal information than necessary.\n\n**Practise consent and discretion.** In a fictional scenario, the affected learner says they are embarrassed that they clicked a misleading link. The helper can reply without blaming them, suggest the provider's recovery process and offer to sit nearby while they contact authorised support. Write down the service name, public support page and next agreed action, not login details. Ask the learner before involving additional classmates. A teacher or safeguarding contact may need information when safety is at risk, but ordinary curiosity is never a reason to circulate private evidence.\n\n**When the friend hesitates.** Suppose the friend is not ready to report a suspicious message but says they did not submit credentials. Explain what may safely be checked through known channels and offer help again without coercion. If they later disclose threats, extortion or pressure to share intimate information, follow a trusted adult or school's safeguarding route promptly. Distinguish ordinary account assistance from a safety concern needing responsible adults. The goal is not for peers to investigate one another; it is to provide support while keeping control and sensitive data with the appropriate person.\n\n**Listen before proposing a fix.** A frightened friend may need a quiet place, company or help explaining the problem to an adult. Ask which of those would help. Avoid demanding proof or asking why they believed the message. A person who feels judged may stop sharing the few facts needed to arrange support. You do not need to inspect private photographs or conversations to believe that someone is upset. If intimate material is involved, do not ask them to send it to you or copy it into a group.\n\n**A safe four-part conversation.** Write a fictional exchange in which the helper first acknowledges distress, then asks what help is wanted, offers a trusted support route and agrees a next step. Try: 'That sounds frightening. You don't need to show me the picture. Would you like me to sit with you while you speak to a trusted adult?' The affected person can describe the concern directly to the appropriate helper. Do not promise absolute secrecy if a serious safety concern means responsible adults need to be involved; explain the reason for seeking help as clearly and kindly as possible.\n\n**Compare two offers.** Offer A is to take the friend's password and answer all messages for them. Offer B is to help locate the platform's official reporting instructions while the friend retains control. Explain which respects agency and why. B offers useful assistance without creating another person with private account access. It does not guarantee the platform will remove content immediately. A may feel efficient, but it can increase exposure, confuse the record of who acted and make the friend dependent on the helper.\n\n**Support after the first report.** Ask what practical support is still wanted: accompanying the friend to a teacher, helping them write down non-secret facts or checking in later. Avoid asking for continual updates for your own curiosity. If classmates begin speculating, do not confirm private details or identify the person in a public warning. Encourage a general message about seeking trusted help instead. Reflection: What if the friend declines ordinary account assistance but describes a credible threat to their safety? Seek a trusted safeguarding adult promptly and explain what you are doing. Helping well protects the person's dignity while recognising when the problem exceeds a peer's responsibility."
    },
    {
      "position": 6,
      "title": "Recovering important files and access",
      "content": "Recovery means restoring an intended capability with appropriate confidence. A password change may not remove an old session; a device restart may not restore deleted work; a successful backup job may not prove restoration works. The right evidence depends on what was lost or affected.\n\nWorked example: After a device problem, a student restores one assignment from a tested backup, checks that the file opens correctly and confirms that their course progress is accessible. They also review whether the recovered account's security settings are correct.\n\n**A safe restoration rehearsal.** Make a disposable text assignment with a distinctive last sentence, save a separate copy and delete only the working example. Record when the saved copy was made, how it was retrieved and whether the final sentence returned. For account recovery, use a fictional scenario and reference a provider's documented support channel; never request genuine classmate passwords or reset tokens. Explain what the learner would report if the file restores but newer edits are missing. A truthful report describes the version recovered, not merely that the document opened.\n\n**When the first fix is insufficient.** Suppose access returns to the student's portal but the assignment is older than the one submitted yesterday. The account recovery can be counted as successful while file recovery remains partial. Check authorized version history or ask school support about approved submission evidence; do not assume an unknown cloud account contains the missing version. Record what can be restored, what must be reconstructed and which future backup rhythm would reduce similar loss. This is a lesson in verification and limits, not an invitation to experiment on live school records.\n\n**Prepare without risking the only copy.** The optional file rehearsal uses a disposable document created for this lesson. If you are unsure how to make a separate copy safely, do the entire task on paper instead: imagine version A says 'draft complete' and version B adds 'teacher comments included'. The distinction is which version comes back, not whether you can perform a clever recovery trick. Never delete an actual assignment, empty a shared folder or reset a real account to make the exercise realistic.\n\n**Use the version facts.** A fictional learner saves a backup at 18:00, adds the teacher's comments at 19:00 and loses access to the working file at 19:10. The restored 18:00 copy opens and contains the earlier draft. Write what recovery succeeded and what is still missing. The correct conclusion is partial file recovery: the old draft returned, but the later comments are not yet accounted for. Opening a file is a useful check, not proof that its contents are current.\n\n**Choose the next legitimate source.** The learner can check supported version history, an authorised submission copy or an offline copy they already control. They should not sign in through a stranger's account promising to recover every version. If a teacher holds the submitted work, ask through the normal school route rather than requesting access to the teacher's private storage. If no newer version exists, record what must be reconstructed and ask about any affected deadline. Honest limits are more useful than repeatedly trying unverified recovery tools.\n\n**Close the two tasks separately.** For an account problem, confirm the rightful learner can use the intended service with its expected protections. For a file problem, confirm which document and version returned and whether it is usable for the assignment. Keep a short recovery note with the source, version and unresolved gap; it should contain no live codes or passwords. Reflection: What if a file is correct but available only from an account you are not authorised to use? The content check passed, but safe access did not. The takeaway is to restore the real activity, identify what remains missing and improve future copies without pretending that every loss can be reversed."
    },
    {
      "position": 7,
      "title": "Learning from a completed response",
      "content": "An after-action review asks what happened, which assumptions proved incorrect, what helped and what should improve next time. It is not a blame session. A school may discover that its support link was hard to find, or a group may learn that forwarding screenshots exposed details unnecessarily.\n\nWorked example: A fake timetable notice spread widely before students verified it. After correction, the study group creates a standard way to confirm announcements and updates its moderator guidance. A month later it tests the process against a fictional notice.\n\n**Measure a real learning outcome.** Prepare two harmless fictional announcements: one appears through the established portal and another arrives through an unfamiliar chat account. Ask learners to state which evidence they checked before acting. Collect only anonymous results and comments about confusing instructions, rather than asking students to surrender actual chat history. Compare outcomes with the published procedure: could a learner using limited data still reach the official channel? Revise the training if the route is unavailable or poorly explained. Record what improvement is expected before repeating the exercise.\n\n**Retest after practice.** Several weeks later, give students a new synthetic notice with a different persuasive claim but the same need for independent verification. If learners now check the official portal before forwarding messages, that is more meaningful than remembering a slogan. If they still follow contact details supplied by the sender, change the teaching activity and the visibility of school guidance. A corrective action should have an owner, a date for retesting and an observable result. Do not declare the school secure because a single quiz returned high scores.\n\n**Reconstruct one useful turning point.** Use this fictional sequence: a timetable screenshot appears at 08:00; a moderator forwards it at 08:03; the school office confirms it is old at 08:20; a correction reaches only one of three class groups at 08:25. The important problem is not simply that somebody believed a screenshot. The correction did not reach everyone who received the first claim. A review that stops at 'be more careful' would miss that coordination failure.\n\n**Turn a finding into an action.** Write a finding about the missing corrections, choose who can update all three groups and set an observable check. For example, the moderator can maintain the approved list of group contacts and record that each received the correction. That is different from demanding access to every learner's private chat. A student can suggest the change, while the teacher or authorised group organiser decides the actual school process. Keep personal details out of the practice record.\n\n**Exercise with an explained result.** Compare two proposed improvements: hold another awareness talk, or add a correction checklist that names all groups receiving the original notice. Which directly addresses the observed gap? The checklist is more closely tied to this finding, provided the team tests whether it is used. A talk may support understanding but does not demonstrate correction delivery. Neither proves every student read the notice. State that limit rather than turning a completed checklist into a guarantee of perfect communication.\n\n**Review what helped as well.** The office's prompt reply made a correction possible. Preserve that useful route instead of redesigning the whole system merely because the incident was uncomfortable. A good review distinguishes what to keep, what to change and what remains unknown. If the office's public number was hard to find, that is a separate improvement with a different owner. Do not blame the first reporter for a missing school contact page.\n\nReflection: After the retest, two groups receive the correction and the third still does not. Can the action be closed because most groups improved? Record the progress and leave the unresolved route open for further work. An after-action review is successful when it makes a specific future decision or handoff easier. It does not need to publish names, award blame or declare the whole school secure."
    },
    {
      "position": 8,
      "title": "Coordinating a classroom digital-safety exercise",
      "content": "**Put the response together.** This final lesson combines recognition, escalation, containment, clear communication, supportive behaviour and recovery. It uses a complete fictional classroom case below. You can work alone by writing each role's answer in turn, or share the roles with a group. No facilitator, suspicious link, real account or live incident is needed. The aim is a coordinated, evidence-based response rather than guessing the cause first.\n\n**The opening case.** At 14:00, class representative Ada sees a forwarded picture claiming that the examination portal closes at 14:30 and that students must pay a 'reopening fee'. The picture supplies a new payment account. The school's established noticeboard says registration closes tomorrow at 16:00 and lists no reopening fee. A second learner, Ben, reports losing his phone on the bus; he does not know whether somebody has used any account on it. These are two reports, not evidence that the same person caused both.\n\nWorked example: Ada records the fee demand and checks the existing school noticeboard. She asks the school office, through its established route, whether the deadline has changed. Ben seeks a trusted adult's help and the relevant provider or school account-support route for the lost device. The group does not broadcast Ben's private details or ask for his passwords. Its warning about the unverified fee does not claim the lost phone caused the message.\n\n**Choose limited roles.** The reporter writes the observations and times. The verifier compares the school's stated deadline and fee information. The support role identifies who can act on Ben's access concern and who can confirm the school notice. The communicator drafts the group update using only facts ready for publication. In solo work, label four sections with those roles. Role names do not grant permission to change school accounts or contact real students. Actual technical changes remain with the rightful owner and authorised support.\n\n**Round one exercise.** Write an initial decision log: which action should students delay, which information is already checked, which authority must respond and which details should remain private? Then draft a three-sentence class advisory. A reasonable answer advises against acting on the unverified payment demand, gives the established noticeboard as the checking route and says the school office has been asked to confirm. It does not declare every school account compromised or promise that the portal will never close.\n\n**Reveal the next facts.** After recording your first answer, read this update: at 14:15 the school office confirms the noticeboard deadline and says the reopening payment is not authorised. At 14:20 Ben's parent finds the phone in a bag left on the bus and returns it. No account misuse has been confirmed. Revise both report entries. The fee advisory can now state the office's confirmation. Recovering the phone changes Ben's situation, but it does not answer every question about account activity; he can follow appropriate official checks with support if concerned.\n\n**Check the handoffs.** The communicator must receive the office's verified answer before publishing it. Ben should receive personal support without becoming the subject of the group warning. Ask whether the corrected advisory reaches the same groups that received the demand. Do not circulate the suspicious payment details as a shortcut to recognition. If a group member already paid, direct them privately toward their payment provider and a trusted adult rather than promising the class can reverse the transfer.\n\nReflection: Identify one decision you changed after the update, one privacy boundary you maintained and one improvement to practise next time. Explain what evidence supports each conclusion. Further reading: NCSC Top tips for staying secure online, https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online , provides optional background on personal safeguards. NCSC Recovering a hacked account, https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/guidance/recovering-a-hacked-account , explains account-recovery considerations; use the affected provider's own instructions and appropriate local school or safety support. Its UK-specific reporting routes are not Nigerian reporting instructions."
    }
  ]
}
